  <ul style="margin-left:20px;">
    <li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>1</span>. Shivaji was crowned as Chhatrapati in 1674 at Raigad Fort.</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>2</span>. The Treaty of Purandar (1665) was mediated by Raja Jai Singh I.</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>3</span>. Baji Rao I is credited with the 'Northward Policy' (Attock to Cuttack).</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>4</span>. The Maratha navy was pioneered by Kanhoji Angre.</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>5</span>. The Third Battle of Panipat occurred in 1761, led by Sadashivrao Bhau.</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>6</span>. Chauth was a protection tax, while Sardeshmukhi was a claim of supremacy.</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>7</span>. The Peshwas shifted the capital from Satara to Pune.</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>8</span>. The First Anglo-Maratha War ended with the Treaty of Salbai (1782).</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>9</span>. The Second Anglo-Maratha War ended with the Treaty of Bassein (1802).</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>10</span>. The Maratha Empire officially collapsed after the Third Anglo-Maratha War (1817-1818).</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>11</span>. Shivaji's administration was based on the 'Ashta Pradhan' council.</li><li><span style='color:var(--warning)'>12</span>. The Marathas utilized 'Hill Forts' as the backbone of their defense strategy.</li>
  </ul>
